Mariya Rosemere 'va is fearless the 67  year old may have trouble walking but  she still been taken to the streets of  Moscow with a relative konstantin every  weekend for nearly two months now even  after the russian police responded with  mass arrests when candidates are  borrowed from elections and basic  democratic rights are violated she says  you have to protest even if I'm half  dead or have to crawl  I'll take part in every protest that can  prevent the destruction of my country  and my people because that's what I'm  seeing here and the propaganda on  television won't stop me which is not  demonstrating Maria spends her days  lying down the former teacher has  rheumatism and is an almost constant  pain  she stopped watching the state TV  channels years ago she says because it's  all lies she prefers critical online  media and opposition bloggers like  politician Alexei Navalny Maria grew up  in a politically active family in the  Soviet Union and had hoped a very  different system would replace it but  she says the current situation in Russia  is just as repressive as the former  regime the government can do whatever it  wants with whomever at once  it can hit anyone at any time they can  take away your property or even your  freedom it can happen to anyone at all  or even to those loyal to the government  the Picasso's are in danger of losing  custody of their youngest son they've  been accused of taking him along to  demonstrations many Russians are shocked  that the government would take such  extreme measures  we love our children very much and we  hope that this case doesn't tear our  family apart over a dozen protesters  have been taken to court for allegedly  inciting mass uprisings at protests in  recent weeks observers say the state is  using the trials as a show of strength  many of these trials give you the  impression they're not prosecuting with  the aim of reaching a verdict they're  prosecuting just so they can arrest  search and interrogate 